<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
     Recommended way to edit .jfc files is to use the configure command of
     the 'jfr' tool, i.e. jfr configure, or JDK Mission Control
     see Window -> Flight Recorder Template Manager
-->

<!--
     sava RPC soak recording settings - validate profile

     Derived from JDK 25.0.2's lib/jfr/default.jfc (the "Continuous", <1 % overhead
     profile).  Every delta below lives in the event's OWN <setting> element: a plain
     -XX:StartFlightRecording:settings=<file> load reads only those and ignores the
     <control> defaults at the bottom of the file (measured on this JDK - a filter
     placed in <control><text name="method-timing"> produced no "New filter installed"
     line at all).  Regenerate rather than hand-edit: soak/README.md names the script.

     This harness drives sava-rpc's own websocket and HTTP clients against controlled
     local peers that inject faults deliberately.  The recording is an artifact people
     attach to issues, so the privacy deltas (A) are on for every profile, not only
     for live-provider runs.

     A. Privacy and per-chunk noise - enabled true -> false
        jdk.InitialEnvironmentVariable  a default recording stores env vars verbatim;
                                        measured SAVA_RPC_API_KEY=... in the .jfr.
        jdk.InitialSystemProperty       same for -D properties.
        jdk.SystemProcess               every command line on the machine
                                        (~2400 rows / 256 KB per chunk).
        jdk.NativeLibrary               ~3100 rows / 278 KB per chunk, static after
                                        startup.
        Together ~530 KB per chunk, which on a small ring is a large fraction of it,
        and two of them are credential exposure.

     B. Transport - the point of the exercise
        jdk.SocketRead / jdk.SocketWrite: stackTrace true -> false,
                                          throttle 100/s -> 500/s,
                                          threshold 1 ms -> 0 ms.
        The throttle, not the threshold, is what caps socket visibility; dropping the
        threshold alone changes nothing above 100 events/s.  Both events do fire for
        java.net.http.HttpClient and for the JDK WebSocket (reads on the
        HttpClient-N-SelectorManager thread, handshake writes on the client executor,
        sendText writes on the calling thread), so per-request byte counts must still
        come from harness counters - the stack is the same three JDK frames every
        time, which is where most of the size saving is.
        0 ms, with the 500/s throttle, is what makes per-frame
        websocket writes visible - this file exists to measure that rate and the socket
        share of the ring before the pilot and campaign choose their tail threshold.

     C. Blocking and contention on the client's own threads - threshold 20 ms -> 10 ms
        jdk.JavaMonitorEnter  fires for virtual threads on 25.0.2 (measured); this is
                              how contention on SolanaJsonRpcWebsocket's connection
                              lock and on HttpClientImpl's per-client monitor shows.
        jdk.JavaMonitorWait   same control.
        jdk.ThreadPark        platform threads only - LockSupport branches to
                              parkVirtualThread before the VM event - so it covers the
                              engine's check-loop thread and the SelectorManager.
        jdk.ThreadSleep       kept enabled: it DOES fire for virtual threads on 25.0.2
                              (Thread.sleepNanos brackets both branches), the one cheap
                              blocking event that survives virtualisation.

     D. Virtual threads
        jdk.VirtualThreadStart  enabled false -> true, stackTrace true -> false
        jdk.VirtualThreadEnd    enabled false -> true
            Balancing starts against ends is how a leaked harness worker is detected;
            the creation site is already known, so the stack is pure cost.
        jdk.VirtualThreadPinned threshold 20 ms -> 1 ms
            The default is 20 ms, not 1 ms.  JEP 491 means monitors no longer pin, so
            on this JDK any hit is a genuine finding (a native or VM frame).
        jdk.VirtualThreadSubmitFailed is already enabled - left alone.

     E. Leak hunt
        jdk.OldObjectSample stackTrace false -> true.  The allocation site is the whole
        value of the event; the referrer chain comes from the dump, not from here.
        cutoff is left alone and memory-leaks=gc-roots is deliberately NOT baked in:
        the end-of-run `jcmd JFR.dump path-to-gc-roots=true` sets cutoff to infinity
        for that dump.  The event exists only at stop/dump, so a mid-run summary of
        the repository can never show a leak, and a count of 0 means the sampler was
        starved - INCONCLUSIVE, never PASS.

     F. Gauges - slow the periodics down
        jdk.NativeMemoryUsage       1000 ms -> 30 s  (needs -XX:NativeMemoryTracking=
        jdk.NativeMemoryUsageTotal  1000 ms -> 30 s   summary, or both emit nothing;
                                                      one row per memory tag per period)
        jdk.ResidentSetSize         1000 ms -> 10 s  (no NMT needed)
        jdk.ThreadDump              everyChunk -> 60 s, and it lists PLATFORM threads
                                    only - virtual-thread leakage needs the
                                    VirtualThreadStart/End balance instead.

     G. Left as default.jfc has them, deliberately
        jdk.JavaExceptionThrow   enabled, throttle 100/s (`exceptions=throttled`).
                                 Never `exceptions=all` in a soak that provokes throws
                                 on purpose.  The Alt-Svc URISyntaxException noise floor
                                 from the JDK 26 notes does not exist on 25.0.2 - there
                                 is no Alt-Svc code in java.net.http at all - so there is
                                 nothing to suppress; the validate run measures this
                                 JVM's own floor instead.
        jdk.ExceptionStatistics  1 s, unthrottled totals: how you know the 100/s
                                 throttle is biting.
        jdk.ExecutionSample      20 ms.  The report must read the `sampledThread` field;
                                 eventThread is null on these and a report that uses it
                                 silently produces an empty hot-thread table.  Virtual
                                 threads appear with javaName='' and osThreadId=-1.
        jdk.MethodTiming         enabled, period endChunk, filter EMPTY here: the filter
                                 goes on the command line, where it also lands in the
                                 recording as a jdk.ActiveSetting row and in the run's
                                 own shell log.  `invocations` is cumulative since
                                 recording start - diff consecutive rows, never sum them.
        jdk.CPUTimeSample        disabled (Linux only; empty on macOS).
        gc / compiler / allocation selections untouched.

     The one delta inside G: jdk.MethodTrace keeps enabled=true and its EMPTY filter but
     its threshold moves 0 ms -> 50 ms.  With a filter at 0 ms it emits one event with a
     stack per call (measured: 200,000 calls -> 200,000 events / 2.9 MB), which an
     8-hour run cannot afford; at 50 ms a targeted `method-trace=<filter>` on the command
     line records only the outliers.

     On balance the deltas remove more than they add: four bulky per-chunk events off and
     three periodics slowed, against a raised socket throttle and two virtual-thread
     lifecycle events.

     H. The harness's own events (sava.soak.*, declared in module software.sava.rpc.soak)
        Custom events in a named module record under settings=default with no explicit
        enable, but naming them here makes the recording self-describing and pins the two
        rates that matter:
          sava.soak.NotificationDelivered  threshold 5 ms, stackTrace true - one per
                                           delivered notification is far too many; the
                                           slow tail with its consumer stack is the point.
          sava.soak.RpcCall                threshold 25 ms - same argument on the HTTP side.
          sava.soak.Gauge                  period 10 s - the periodic gauge, committed
                                           alongside the client.csv row.
          sava.soak.Misattribution         stackTrace true - the rarest event in the run
                                           and the one whose stack is the evidence.
        Everything else is low-rate by construction and simply enabled.
-->

<configuration version="2.0" label="Continuous" description="Low overhead configuration safe for continuous use in production environments, typically less than 1 % overhead." provider="Oracle">

    <event name="jdk.ResidentSetSize">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="period">10 s</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.ThreadAllocationStatistics">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="period">everyChunk</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.ClassLoadingStatistics">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="period">1000 ms</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.ClassLoaderStatistics">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="period">everyChunk</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.JavaThreadStatistics">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="period">1000 ms</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.SymbolTableStatistics">
      <setting name="enabled">false</setting>
      <setting name="period">10 s</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.StringTableStatistics">
      <setting name="enabled">false</setting>
      <setting name="period">10 s</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.ThreadStart">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">true</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.ThreadEnd">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.ThreadSleep">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">true</setting>
      <setting name="threshold" control="locking-threshold">10 ms</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.ThreadPark">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">true</setting>
      <setting name="threshold" control="locking-threshold">10 ms</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.VirtualThreadStart">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">false</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.VirtualThreadEnd">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.VirtualThreadPinned">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">true</setting>
      <setting name="threshold">1 ms</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.VirtualThreadSubmitFailed">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">true</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.JavaMonitorEnter">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">true</setting>
      <setting name="threshold" control="locking-threshold">10 ms</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.JavaMonitorWait">
      <setting name="enabled">true</setting>
      <setting name="stackTrace">true</setting>
      <setting name="threshold" control="locking-threshold">10 ms</setting>
    </event>

    <event name="jdk.JavaMonitorNotify">
      <setting name="enabled">false</setting>
